# CODEOWNERS — Aframp Backend
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owners listed here whenever a pull request
# touches a matching path. This adds a *human* review layer on top of the
# automated `Guard` CI job that already blocks edits to protected paths
# (see CONTRIBUTING.md and .github/workflows/ci.yml).
#
# Syntax reference: https://docs.github.com/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule order matters: the LAST matching pattern wins.
# Keep the most specific rules at the BOTTOM of each section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default owner — everything not matched below
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*                               @kelly-musk

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Critical source: security-sensitive / money-moving code.
#
# These paths are also blocked from direct edits by the CI Guard job, so the
# only way they change is a deliberate maintainer-authored merge. CODEOWNERS
# guarantees a named human is on every such merge.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Authentication, sessions, JWT and password handling.
src/auth/                      @kelly-musk

# Stellar integration: listener, worker, key handling and wallet crypto.
src/blockchain/                @kelly-musk

# Wallet encryption / key derivation helpers (explicit for discoverability,
# even though already covered by src/blockchain/).
src/blockchain/wallet_crypto.rs @kelly-musk

# Database schema. Every migration is a one-way door on live data.
migrations/                    @kelly-musk

# Withdrawal, payment and payout processing — direct custodial fund movement.
src/services/withdrawals.rs    @kelly-musk
src/services/payments.rs       @kelly-musk
src/services/payment_requests.rs @kelly-musk

# OTP issuance, hashing and phone verification.
src/services/otp.rs            @kelly-musk
src/otp/                       @kelly-musk

# Webhook ingress and signature verification.
src/api/webhooks.rs            @kelly-musk

# Process entrypoint and shared wiring.
src/main.rs                    @kelly-musk
src/lib.rs                     @kelly-musk

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CI, release and security policy files.
#
# A change here can weaken the guardrails for every future contribution, so it
# gets the same treatment as production source.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.github/workflows/              @kelly-musk
.github/CODEOWNERS             @kelly-musk
SECURITY.md                    @kelly-musk
CONTRIBUTING.md                @kelly-musk

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Contributor sandbox.
#
# `tests/`, `docs/` and `scripts/` are the paths open-source contributors may
# edit freely. They still warrant a light review, but they are NOT part of the
# critical set, so they fall back to the default owner above.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Explicit note for reviewers — these are the only paths the CI Guard permits
# contributors to change on `dev-backend`:
#   tests/
#   docs/
#   scripts/
#   API.md
#   openapi.yaml
#   README.md
#   CHANGELOG.md
